WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.604 --> 00:00:09.450
DAVID NETTLETON: Hi then,
and welcome to this session

00:00:09.450 --> 00:00:14.970
on where to store your stuff
with Google Cloud Platform.

00:00:14.970 --> 00:00:18.210
My name is David Nettleton.

00:00:18.210 --> 00:00:21.540
I'm a product manager
here at Google Cloud.

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:24.130
I've been here for
about four years now,

00:00:24.130 --> 00:00:28.020
and I've been working on
storage throughout that time.

00:00:28.020 --> 00:00:31.230
And what I'd like to do today is
cover two main topics, really.

00:00:31.230 --> 00:00:35.490
So first of all, just maybe
stand back a little bit

00:00:35.490 --> 00:00:39.120
and go through a history
of how storage has evolved,

00:00:39.120 --> 00:00:43.500
how databases have evolved, and
then walk through from there

00:00:43.500 --> 00:00:46.080
some of the options that
are available in Google

00:00:46.080 --> 00:00:50.140
Cloud for storing your data.

00:00:50.140 --> 00:00:53.760
Before I get to that, though,
a quick safe-harbor statement.

00:00:53.760 --> 00:00:57.510
Just to remind folks that some
of the announcements that I

00:00:57.510 --> 00:01:01.770
talk about in here
are forward looking

00:01:01.770 --> 00:01:06.330
and bear in mind from a
safe-harbors perspective.

00:01:06.330 --> 00:01:10.620
So first of all, let me start
with a history of storage.

00:01:10.620 --> 00:01:14.890
And if I go back
30, 40, 50 years,

00:01:14.890 --> 00:01:17.640
the way that storage
first came about

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:20.770
was really storage physically
attached to the computer,

00:01:20.770 --> 00:01:21.270
right?

00:01:21.270 --> 00:01:24.542
So on the left-hand
side here, there's

00:01:24.542 --> 00:01:26.500
this notion of sort of
direct-attached storage.

00:01:26.500 --> 00:01:28.440
Storage is physically
attached to the computer

00:01:28.440 --> 00:01:29.690
that you're using.

00:01:29.690 --> 00:01:31.410
The storage is really
there only to be

00:01:31.410 --> 00:01:32.880
used for the local machine.

00:01:32.880 --> 00:01:36.060
And as the local data, and it's
all very physically connected,

00:01:36.060 --> 00:01:38.967
often into a single piece
of hardware with a monitor

00:01:38.967 --> 00:01:40.300
that somebody would then sit at.

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:42.250
And we're going back
a long way for this,

00:01:42.250 --> 00:01:44.250
but this is classic
direct-attached storage.

00:01:44.250 --> 00:01:49.330
Also, a common PC would
also look like this as well.

00:01:49.330 --> 00:01:53.100
But then as people
wanted to think

00:01:53.100 --> 00:01:55.380
about sharing their
data more, they

00:01:55.380 --> 00:01:58.050
started to think about what
does data and storage look

00:01:58.050 --> 00:02:00.182
like over a network?

00:02:00.182 --> 00:02:02.140
And this evolved in a
number of different ways.

00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:08.370
So one way in which this
evolved is to take a storage,

00:02:08.370 --> 00:02:11.100
move just the storage
over a network,

00:02:11.100 --> 00:02:14.580
leave the file system local
to the machine or the computer

00:02:14.580 --> 00:02:18.000
on which you are using,
and have block storage be

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:19.200
remote over the network.

00:02:19.200 --> 00:02:21.570
Block storage was
the preferred model

00:02:21.570 --> 00:02:26.480
here because it would give
good remote performance.

00:02:26.480 --> 00:02:29.220
The file system would be
local on the computer.

00:02:29.220 --> 00:02:34.410
It would use large block
sizes, 8K, 16k block sizes,

00:02:34.410 --> 00:02:37.230
or even larger, 64K,
to communicate remotely

00:02:37.230 --> 00:02:40.950
over the network sending large
I/O requests to bring data

00:02:40.950 --> 00:02:42.450
back and forward,
and it would cache

00:02:42.450 --> 00:02:45.730
what it would need locally.

00:02:45.730 --> 00:02:48.690
And this really was the area of
sort of storage area networks

00:02:48.690 --> 00:02:52.860
where companies could start
to consolidate storage

00:02:52.860 --> 00:02:55.680
from individual machines,
started consolidated

00:02:55.680 --> 00:02:58.170
into a central pool of
storage, and managed

00:02:58.170 --> 00:03:01.770
high-performance block
storage centrally

00:03:01.770 --> 00:03:04.680
and deliver it to the
applications that needed it

00:03:04.680 --> 00:03:07.010
over the network.

00:03:07.010 --> 00:03:09.510
So that was pretty good for
some of the high-performance use

00:03:09.510 --> 00:03:14.850
cases where you need the local
performance on the machine,

00:03:14.850 --> 00:03:16.525
but what it didn't
work so well is

00:03:16.525 --> 00:03:21.180
is if you wanted to share
files between different users

00:03:21.180 --> 00:03:22.280
in an organization.

00:03:22.280 --> 00:03:24.240
And for that, the
classic file share

00:03:24.240 --> 00:03:27.963
of the network-attached
storage was the storage option

00:03:27.963 --> 00:03:28.880
that evolved for that.

00:03:28.880 --> 00:03:32.300
So it's file storage, file
sharing over a network.

00:03:32.300 --> 00:03:36.510
Here, the file system is remote.

00:03:36.510 --> 00:03:39.650
It's across the network
from the compute

00:03:39.650 --> 00:03:41.640
or the application
that's using it.

00:03:41.640 --> 00:03:45.150
And the file system is
available to all users

00:03:45.150 --> 00:03:49.500
to be able to connect to
it, use it, and share it.

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:51.623
That's great from a
sharing perspective.

00:03:51.623 --> 00:03:53.290
One of the challenges
with that, though,

00:03:53.290 --> 00:03:56.970
is as customers start to--
as users in an organization

00:03:56.970 --> 00:03:59.610
start to share and
use many files,

00:03:59.610 --> 00:04:03.240
then lots of complexities
start to arise in terms

00:04:03.240 --> 00:04:06.010
of who can access what file.

00:04:06.010 --> 00:04:07.110
Who's allowed to read it?

00:04:07.110 --> 00:04:08.880
Who can write it?

00:04:08.880 --> 00:04:11.250
What are the
permissions around that?

00:04:11.250 --> 00:04:14.610
And then managing
folders and hierarchies

00:04:14.610 --> 00:04:16.760
and scaling the
metadata storage means

00:04:16.760 --> 00:04:22.110
building large-scale,
scalable, high-performance file

00:04:22.110 --> 00:04:27.420
systems is a really big,
hard, challenging problem

00:04:27.420 --> 00:04:31.740
but super powerful in that
it's very easy to then share

00:04:31.740 --> 00:04:36.870
file data between many
different applications.

00:04:36.870 --> 00:04:41.580
So that was really how storage
evolved for very many years.

00:04:41.580 --> 00:04:43.580
And then a final
class of storage

00:04:43.580 --> 00:04:45.660
has emerged more
recently and actually

00:04:45.660 --> 00:04:50.300
probably popularized
in the cloud

00:04:50.300 --> 00:04:52.990
first and foremost,
which was object storage.

00:04:52.990 --> 00:04:56.010
So here the goal is to
be able to store really

00:04:56.010 --> 00:04:59.080
large amounts of
unstructured data.

00:04:59.080 --> 00:05:04.320
So think videos or
pictures, images,

00:05:04.320 --> 00:05:08.833
store it really cheaply and
at really, really large scale.

00:05:08.833 --> 00:05:11.000
As I said, that's something
that really sort of came

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:12.830
to fruition in the
public cloud first where

00:05:12.830 --> 00:05:15.650
customers would look
for a cheap, easy way

00:05:15.650 --> 00:05:18.830
to store flexibly
large amounts of data

00:05:18.830 --> 00:05:22.850
and then access it, often
occasionally, to get access

00:05:22.850 --> 00:05:23.420
to that.

00:05:23.420 --> 00:05:26.810
And this model made some
very big trade-offs, right?

00:05:26.810 --> 00:05:29.600
It had a very simple API.

00:05:29.600 --> 00:05:32.000
There wasn't a lot of sharing
between users of the data.

00:05:32.000 --> 00:05:34.130
It's often append only.

00:05:34.130 --> 00:05:36.410
And it made trade-offs
to be able to support

00:05:36.410 --> 00:05:40.760
that storing large
amounts of data remotely

00:05:40.760 --> 00:05:44.690
and being able to access
it in a very simple API.

00:05:44.690 --> 00:05:46.580
And a new API, which
was the object API,

00:05:46.580 --> 00:05:50.450
was brought about
in order to do that.

00:05:50.450 --> 00:05:53.660
So if I'm at these sort of
different classes or types

00:05:53.660 --> 00:05:56.520
of storage now to what is
available in the cloud,

00:05:56.520 --> 00:06:00.740
the evolution of
the classic storage

00:06:00.740 --> 00:06:04.340
being tightly coupled to the
application and the file system

00:06:04.340 --> 00:06:07.560
and compute is available--
so this is classic block

00:06:07.560 --> 00:06:10.910
storage-- is available in two
forms in Google Cloud Platform.

00:06:10.910 --> 00:06:14.150
First of all, we have Persistent
Disk in a standard and SSD

00:06:14.150 --> 00:06:18.200
format which lets
you remotely mount

00:06:18.200 --> 00:06:22.190
a block-storage device or a
disk at your VM in Google Cloud

00:06:22.190 --> 00:06:24.080
Platform.

00:06:24.080 --> 00:06:30.500
A disk is durable, able to
withstand restarts of the VM,

00:06:30.500 --> 00:06:33.540
and high performant.

00:06:33.540 --> 00:06:35.540
In addition, we have
a product called

00:06:35.540 --> 00:06:38.750
Local SSD which is
ephemeral storage,

00:06:38.750 --> 00:06:42.470
is attached very much directly
to the actual physical compute

00:06:42.470 --> 00:06:43.520
instance that is running.

00:06:43.520 --> 00:06:46.620
So you can get much, much
higher performance from it,

00:06:46.620 --> 00:06:47.850
but it's ephemeral.

00:06:47.850 --> 00:06:50.450
So those are the two
block-storage solutions that we

00:06:50.450 --> 00:06:52.600
have available in Google Cloud.

00:06:52.600 --> 00:06:55.370
For network-attached
storage or a file share

00:06:55.370 --> 00:06:58.305
where I want to have
a file share which

00:06:58.305 --> 00:07:01.730
is shared in the department
or across my enterprise,

00:07:01.730 --> 00:07:04.430
from Google Cloud we have
Google Cloud Filestore

00:07:04.430 --> 00:07:06.000
as our first-party product.

00:07:06.000 --> 00:07:09.740
And then we also work
closely with partners

00:07:09.740 --> 00:07:14.840
such as NetApp, Dell EMC, in
particular their Isilon product

00:07:14.840 --> 00:07:16.240
and DDN.

00:07:16.240 --> 00:07:19.190
And these are
enterprise vendors who

00:07:19.190 --> 00:07:22.640
have been working in the
area of file storage for very

00:07:22.640 --> 00:07:25.070
many years now,
and many customers

00:07:25.070 --> 00:07:26.630
have been working with them.

00:07:26.630 --> 00:07:29.630
We want to make sure we can help
carry those customers forward

00:07:29.630 --> 00:07:32.260
as they look to use those
products in the cloud.

00:07:32.260 --> 00:07:34.970
And then for object storage
in Google Cloud, the product

00:07:34.970 --> 00:07:37.010
we have there is
called Cloud Storage.

00:07:37.010 --> 00:07:38.960
That's our
object-storage product

00:07:38.960 --> 00:07:41.330
which lets you store data
at really large scale.

00:07:41.330 --> 00:07:45.650
I'll go through in detail
some more on the Google Cloud

00:07:45.650 --> 00:07:50.430
products that we have available
later in the presentation.

00:07:50.430 --> 00:07:53.960
So let me also talk about
other places in Google Cloud

00:07:53.960 --> 00:07:54.820
that you might--

00:07:54.820 --> 00:07:57.890
or products in Google Cloud that
you might use to store data.

00:07:57.890 --> 00:08:00.280
Let's talk about databases.

00:08:00.280 --> 00:08:05.710
And so at a high level, for many
years the world of databases,

00:08:05.710 --> 00:08:07.460
there was sort of a
very traditional model

00:08:07.460 --> 00:08:08.670
that lasted for many years.

00:08:08.670 --> 00:08:11.540
So it's sort of the
late '60s, '70s,

00:08:11.540 --> 00:08:15.050
the classic relational
database where you would

00:08:15.050 --> 00:08:18.500
store a record in a table.

00:08:18.500 --> 00:08:19.980
A row would be
stored in a table,

00:08:19.980 --> 00:08:21.813
and you can join it
together with other rows

00:08:21.813 --> 00:08:22.700
and other tables.

00:08:22.700 --> 00:08:27.860
Really introduced a very
powerful model for managing,

00:08:27.860 --> 00:08:31.850
storing, and
reasoning about data.

00:08:31.850 --> 00:08:35.000
And the traditional
relational-database-management

00:08:35.000 --> 00:08:40.669
market grew to be a very,
very large business with IBM

00:08:40.669 --> 00:08:43.760
and Oracle leading
the way and grew

00:08:43.760 --> 00:08:47.480
into a very large, traditional,
on-premises industry.

00:08:50.840 --> 00:08:53.750
The benefit of this was that
you could you really fast

00:08:53.750 --> 00:08:57.650
reasoning over small amounts of
data, store it pretty quickly.

00:08:57.650 --> 00:08:59.910
If you wanted to run large
queries for analytics,

00:08:59.910 --> 00:09:03.050
do reporting or
run a dashboard, it

00:09:03.050 --> 00:09:05.840
was normally pretty bad
practice to run those queries

00:09:05.840 --> 00:09:08.840
against the same system
that's running your banking

00:09:08.840 --> 00:09:10.340
application.

00:09:10.340 --> 00:09:14.720
So quite often data would
be moved off of the database

00:09:14.720 --> 00:09:19.190
system into a data warehouse
via a process known as ETL,

00:09:19.190 --> 00:09:22.190
Extract, Transform, Load.

00:09:22.190 --> 00:09:26.540
And data would be copied
over into a data warehouse.

00:09:26.540 --> 00:09:28.430
It would be rearranged
in a particular way

00:09:28.430 --> 00:09:31.040
to make it really easy to
do large-scale analytics

00:09:31.040 --> 00:09:32.820
and reporting.

00:09:32.820 --> 00:09:35.690
And that combination of
database and data warehouse

00:09:35.690 --> 00:09:38.930
really has stood the test
of time for many years,

00:09:38.930 --> 00:09:44.150
and it still remains the bastion
of many sort of enterprise

00:09:44.150 --> 00:09:50.850
organizations' data storage
and analytics processes.

00:09:50.850 --> 00:09:55.600
And so, really, that ecosystem
existed for a very long time.

00:09:55.600 --> 00:10:01.050
And then over towards the
start of-- through the 2000s,

00:10:01.050 --> 00:10:06.330
there was a whole number
of different trends

00:10:06.330 --> 00:10:10.690
that started to affect how well
that ecosystem could scale.

00:10:10.690 --> 00:10:13.680
So first and foremost was
the emergence of just really

00:10:13.680 --> 00:10:16.290
large amounts of
unstructured data.

00:10:16.290 --> 00:10:18.840
With more devices connected,
with the emergence

00:10:18.840 --> 00:10:24.150
of the internet, with different
types of data being generated,

00:10:24.150 --> 00:10:26.225
the traditional
relational-database management

00:10:26.225 --> 00:10:27.600
system just simply
could not cope

00:10:27.600 --> 00:10:29.760
with that amount
of storage anymore

00:10:29.760 --> 00:10:32.400
and the amount of analytics
that needed to run over it.

00:10:32.400 --> 00:10:35.280
So several types of
more specialized systems

00:10:35.280 --> 00:10:36.450
started to emerge.

00:10:36.450 --> 00:10:38.280
Probably one of the
most famous of those

00:10:38.280 --> 00:10:41.670
is Hadoop, which is a
large-scale analytics

00:10:41.670 --> 00:10:43.710
platform for
unstructured data that

00:10:43.710 --> 00:10:46.980
lets you get a large amounts
of data really cheaply,

00:10:46.980 --> 00:10:50.670
run large amounts of
batched processing over it,

00:10:50.670 --> 00:10:55.460
and do analysis on that.

00:10:55.460 --> 00:10:57.870
And that really
changed the dynamic

00:10:57.870 --> 00:10:59.720
of how people thought
about doing analytics,

00:10:59.720 --> 00:11:00.790
and there was a big debate.

00:11:00.790 --> 00:11:02.490
Should I get rid of my
warehouse and put everything

00:11:02.490 --> 00:11:03.290
in a data lake?

00:11:03.290 --> 00:11:05.053
Or everything should
go in the data lake,

00:11:05.053 --> 00:11:06.720
or everything goes
in my data warehouse.

00:11:06.720 --> 00:11:08.610
And in reality, both
of these systems

00:11:08.610 --> 00:11:11.580
are perfectly good at
working side by side,

00:11:11.580 --> 00:11:13.590
and often customers
will store data

00:11:13.590 --> 00:11:15.660
in a data lake for
their initial capture

00:11:15.660 --> 00:11:18.910
and then load it eventually
into a warehouse.

00:11:18.910 --> 00:11:24.930
But Hadoop came along as
a really interesting trend

00:11:24.930 --> 00:11:28.740
and set of capabilities to help
customers more easily get value

00:11:28.740 --> 00:11:31.553
out of their unstructured data.

00:11:31.553 --> 00:11:33.720
Within the more semistructured
and structured world,

00:11:33.720 --> 00:11:35.440
two other things also
happened as well.

00:11:35.440 --> 00:11:38.580
So customers would
also look for, hey, I

00:11:38.580 --> 00:11:43.380
got a really large amount of
sparse data, so key values.

00:11:43.380 --> 00:11:44.820
I want to do lookups.

00:11:44.820 --> 00:11:48.755
I'm gathering records of
data which is semistructured,

00:11:48.755 --> 00:11:50.880
and I've got a really large
amount of it coming out

00:11:50.880 --> 00:11:53.590
of my IoT system, for example.

00:11:53.590 --> 00:11:56.910
Like cell-tower data is
pretty well structured.

00:11:56.910 --> 00:12:01.090
There's 15, 16 different types
of data maybe in a record,

00:12:01.090 --> 00:12:03.390
but that's evolving over time.

00:12:03.390 --> 00:12:09.455
There's really large amounts of
sparse key-value lookup data.

00:12:09.455 --> 00:12:10.830
And that, again,
is not something

00:12:10.830 --> 00:12:13.290
that's particularly well suited
for a traditional database

00:12:13.290 --> 00:12:16.770
system because of the scale
that it wants to operate at

00:12:16.770 --> 00:12:20.730
and the cost trade-offs take
it in a slightly different

00:12:20.730 --> 00:12:21.610
direction.

00:12:21.610 --> 00:12:26.910
And then in addition,
we also saw the trend

00:12:26.910 --> 00:12:29.640
to more semistructured
data storage, things

00:12:29.640 --> 00:12:31.380
like being able to
store JSON documents

00:12:31.380 --> 00:12:35.370
and do reasoning over those as
people started to capture data

00:12:35.370 --> 00:12:37.170
in JSON formats.

00:12:37.170 --> 00:12:39.450
And so they led to
another two types

00:12:39.450 --> 00:12:41.760
of databases, which are
key-value databases and then

00:12:41.760 --> 00:12:46.950
hierarchical databases as two
other kind of classic NoSQL

00:12:46.950 --> 00:12:47.650
databases.

00:12:47.650 --> 00:12:49.620
So this is the
category of databases

00:12:49.620 --> 00:12:56.760
that they don't have often the
same deep traditional semantics

00:12:56.760 --> 00:13:00.990
of the acid properties of a
traditional relational-database

00:13:00.990 --> 00:13:04.830
management systems, and they're
willing to make trade-offs

00:13:04.830 --> 00:13:08.370
in terms of their scale
and other factors in order

00:13:08.370 --> 00:13:13.150
to better support reasoning
over different forms of data.

00:13:13.150 --> 00:13:15.960
And then finally
and most recently,

00:13:15.960 --> 00:13:19.260
we're also now seeing a class
of databases that are really

00:13:19.260 --> 00:13:23.150
large scale, scale-out
relational-database management

00:13:23.150 --> 00:13:23.650
system.

00:13:23.650 --> 00:13:25.920
So the traditional
relational-database management

00:13:25.920 --> 00:13:27.700
system is a scale-up system.

00:13:27.700 --> 00:13:31.470
So you'll put your
database on a server.

00:13:31.470 --> 00:13:33.730
You'll attach a load
of storage to it.

00:13:33.730 --> 00:13:35.467
And if you want to
store more records

00:13:35.467 --> 00:13:37.050
or you want to do
more compute, you've

00:13:37.050 --> 00:13:39.670
got to add more processors
to that same machine,

00:13:39.670 --> 00:13:43.350
or you've got to add more
disks to that array, right?

00:13:43.350 --> 00:13:45.870
And so it was a classic
scale-up system.

00:13:45.870 --> 00:13:48.570
And that runs into
some challenges.

00:13:48.570 --> 00:13:51.330
Customers are buying
bigger and bigger systems

00:13:51.330 --> 00:13:55.200
to operate at larger
and larger scale,

00:13:55.200 --> 00:13:59.910
and that becomes very expensive
and not always very flexible

00:13:59.910 --> 00:14:01.900
in terms of where
you can deploy that.

00:14:01.900 --> 00:14:04.980
You can only deploy it
in a single location.

00:14:04.980 --> 00:14:09.810
So now more recently there's
a class of database systems

00:14:09.810 --> 00:14:16.580
which let you now store records
transactionally at global scale

00:14:16.580 --> 00:14:24.420
and manage really large-scale
transactional database systems.

00:14:24.420 --> 00:14:26.910
If I go through the
Google Cloud products that

00:14:26.910 --> 00:14:29.140
are available across
this portfolio,

00:14:29.140 --> 00:14:30.960
so first of all, the
traditional database

00:14:30.960 --> 00:14:34.070
we have the Cloud
SQL set of products

00:14:34.070 --> 00:14:36.360
for MySQL, Postgres,
and SQL Server that let

00:14:36.360 --> 00:14:37.980
you run and manage a database.

00:14:37.980 --> 00:14:41.040
BigQuery is our
data-warehouse product.

00:14:41.040 --> 00:14:44.910
For a data lake, Google Cloud
Dataproc and Cloud Storage

00:14:44.910 --> 00:14:48.750
give you Dataproc
for managing compute,

00:14:48.750 --> 00:14:51.980
and Cloud Storage
is your data lake.

00:14:51.980 --> 00:14:56.770
And then Bigtable provides
key-value storage files,

00:14:56.770 --> 00:15:01.400
Filestore for hierarchical
data, and then Cloud Spanner

00:15:01.400 --> 00:15:04.560
is our database for
really large-scale,

00:15:04.560 --> 00:15:08.120
global transactional databases.

00:15:08.120 --> 00:15:10.920
So with that overview
in mind, I just

00:15:10.920 --> 00:15:12.960
thought I'd have a
slide that gives you

00:15:12.960 --> 00:15:15.930
a rough mapping of the different
types of storage options

00:15:15.930 --> 00:15:17.760
that are available
to you, depending

00:15:17.760 --> 00:15:20.130
on what it is that
you are looking

00:15:20.130 --> 00:15:23.930
to do with Google Cloud.

00:15:23.930 --> 00:15:25.757
Now let me maybe
just dive into sort

00:15:25.757 --> 00:15:27.340
of the ones that are
on the right side

00:15:27.340 --> 00:15:29.470
there, the storage products.

00:15:29.470 --> 00:15:33.287
So I'll leave the database
overviews to other sessions,

00:15:33.287 --> 00:15:35.120
and I'm just going to
now concentrate and go

00:15:35.120 --> 00:15:36.787
through some of the
storage options that

00:15:36.787 --> 00:15:38.630
are available on GCP.

00:15:38.630 --> 00:15:40.250
So first of all,
Google Cloud Storage.

00:15:40.250 --> 00:15:44.060
So this is our large-scale
object-storage product.

00:15:44.060 --> 00:15:46.790
Customers are normally using
this for three different use

00:15:46.790 --> 00:15:48.090
cases.

00:15:48.090 --> 00:15:49.530
So content storage
and delivery--

00:15:49.530 --> 00:15:52.610
so if you want to store large
amounts of media, video,

00:15:52.610 --> 00:15:57.980
images, and serve
them out to customers

00:15:57.980 --> 00:16:00.180
or store them for
later analysis.

00:16:00.180 --> 00:16:03.020
Google Cloud Storage is a
really great product for that.

00:16:03.020 --> 00:16:05.390
Customers like Spotify use
this for storing and serving

00:16:05.390 --> 00:16:07.850
media content, for example.

00:16:07.850 --> 00:16:10.850
Backup, archival, DR--
so a very common use case

00:16:10.850 --> 00:16:14.630
where you have a set
of data on premises

00:16:14.630 --> 00:16:16.010
that you're looking to protect.

00:16:16.010 --> 00:16:20.120
You want to store it
remotely on a different media

00:16:20.120 --> 00:16:23.060
to protect yourself
against a disaster, either

00:16:23.060 --> 00:16:26.000
a physical disaster or
someone accidentally

00:16:26.000 --> 00:16:27.650
deleting some data
on premises, and you

00:16:27.650 --> 00:16:30.892
want to be able to recover
it via a backup process.

00:16:30.892 --> 00:16:32.600
And so Google Cloud
Storage is integrated

00:16:32.600 --> 00:16:36.600
with very many backup and DR
products from our partners

00:16:36.600 --> 00:16:44.740
and is available to use for
backup, archival, and DR.

00:16:44.740 --> 00:16:48.240
And then the final use case is
analytics, big data, and ML.

00:16:48.240 --> 00:16:50.060
So I talked about
the use case where

00:16:50.060 --> 00:16:51.602
customers are wanting
to store really

00:16:51.602 --> 00:16:53.970
large amounts of unstructured
data for processing,

00:16:53.970 --> 00:16:55.750
and they want to
store that cheaply.

00:16:55.750 --> 00:16:57.170
And the cloud is a
great place to do that.

00:16:57.170 --> 00:16:59.170
They can use something
like Google Cloud Storage

00:16:59.170 --> 00:17:01.290
to store the data super cheaply.

00:17:01.290 --> 00:17:02.810
And then because
compute and storage

00:17:02.810 --> 00:17:07.255
is separate from each other,
customers can store their data.

00:17:07.255 --> 00:17:09.800
And when they want to run
a Hadoop cluster over it,

00:17:09.800 --> 00:17:11.332
they can just spin
that cluster up,

00:17:11.332 --> 00:17:13.290
run it for the amount of
time that it's needed,

00:17:13.290 --> 00:17:14.207
and then shut it down.

00:17:14.207 --> 00:17:19.319
And this is a huge value over
the alternative on premises,

00:17:19.319 --> 00:17:21.829
which is often trying to
size compute and storage

00:17:21.829 --> 00:17:26.119
together in a single
piece of equipment, often

00:17:26.119 --> 00:17:29.600
having to deal with
planning for peak capacity

00:17:29.600 --> 00:17:35.240
and then trying to run it to
make sure that in quiet times

00:17:35.240 --> 00:17:37.580
that you're not
wasting capacity.

00:17:37.580 --> 00:17:40.700
So it takes away a
lot of the challenges

00:17:40.700 --> 00:17:46.340
with trying to optimize the
TCO for a cluster on premises.

00:17:46.340 --> 00:17:49.130
So with the cloud, you can
separate compute from storage.

00:17:49.130 --> 00:17:50.720
Use only what you
need on storage.

00:17:50.720 --> 00:17:52.460
Use only what you
need for compute,

00:17:52.460 --> 00:17:54.893
and run those independently.

00:17:54.893 --> 00:17:56.810
And the product we have,
Google Cloud Storage,

00:17:56.810 --> 00:17:59.410
super powerful for
those use cases.

00:17:59.410 --> 00:18:01.595
A couple of things I'll
highlight there with this

00:18:01.595 --> 00:18:04.220
is we have a number of different
tiers from Standard, Nearline,

00:18:04.220 --> 00:18:07.050
Coldline, all the
way down to Archival.

00:18:07.050 --> 00:18:08.810
And these just give
you different ways

00:18:08.810 --> 00:18:11.912
to pay for how you
store your data.

00:18:11.912 --> 00:18:14.120
Something that's really
interesting about the product

00:18:14.120 --> 00:18:16.520
and very differentiated
is that across all

00:18:16.520 --> 00:18:20.160
of those different
tiers, it's the same API

00:18:20.160 --> 00:18:22.190
and the same access
speeds to the data.

00:18:22.190 --> 00:18:25.370
So you don't need to change
anything in your application

00:18:25.370 --> 00:18:28.110
to be able to take advantage
of tiering data down

00:18:28.110 --> 00:18:32.590
to cheaper and cheaper
storage classes

00:18:32.590 --> 00:18:35.460
if the data just
needs to be accessed.

00:18:35.460 --> 00:18:37.640
But if all of a
sudden that kitten

00:18:37.640 --> 00:18:40.580
movie that was super
popular five years ago

00:18:40.580 --> 00:18:42.380
suddenly picks up
again, well, it's

00:18:42.380 --> 00:18:45.215
right there immediately
available for access

00:18:45.215 --> 00:18:46.840
even if it might be
in an archive tier.

00:18:46.840 --> 00:18:50.750
There's no staging it back
from a different storage

00:18:50.750 --> 00:18:52.250
product that has
to bring it back

00:18:52.250 --> 00:18:56.670
to take a few hours to stage
it back to your main storage

00:18:56.670 --> 00:18:58.730
tiers.

00:18:58.730 --> 00:19:03.560
So that's a couple of benefits
for storage in general.

00:19:03.560 --> 00:19:05.650
And then a few other things
specific to use cases.

00:19:05.650 --> 00:19:09.565
So we have for common
storage and delivery,

00:19:09.565 --> 00:19:12.410
we globally edge cache data
and accelerate uploads.

00:19:12.410 --> 00:19:15.410
So data coming in from
Europe and stored in the US.

00:19:15.410 --> 00:19:17.270
We'll bring it onto the
Google Cloud network

00:19:17.270 --> 00:19:19.228
as soon as possible and
carry it on the network

00:19:19.228 --> 00:19:21.960
for as long as possible
until we serve it.

00:19:21.960 --> 00:19:24.590
For big data and
analytics, for Hadoop,

00:19:24.590 --> 00:19:31.440
all of the different readers
in GCS will see the same data.

00:19:31.440 --> 00:19:35.000
So one of the great features
of Google Cloud Storage

00:19:35.000 --> 00:19:36.440
is that it actually
uses Spanner,

00:19:36.440 --> 00:19:38.660
which I talked about
is a transactionally

00:19:38.660 --> 00:19:41.760
consistent database to
store all of our metadata.

00:19:41.760 --> 00:19:46.040
So when somebody writes
data, everybody else

00:19:46.040 --> 00:19:49.850
can see the same data, which
is a big benefit for Google's

00:19:49.850 --> 00:19:51.115
product here.

00:19:51.115 --> 00:19:52.490
That's actually
pretty hard to do

00:19:52.490 --> 00:19:55.830
and something that's
super powerful.

00:19:55.830 --> 00:19:57.830
And then as I talked about
a little bit earlier,

00:19:57.830 --> 00:19:59.360
the backup, DR, archival.

00:19:59.360 --> 00:20:00.860
Across all our
storage classes, you

00:20:00.860 --> 00:20:02.720
get instant access to the data.

00:20:02.720 --> 00:20:04.940
No need to stage access
to the recovery and deal

00:20:04.940 --> 00:20:08.870
with complicated workflows
as to how you're going

00:20:08.870 --> 00:20:10.560
to get access to your data.

00:20:10.560 --> 00:20:12.572
And then we have a
couple of products

00:20:12.572 --> 00:20:14.030
in the multiregional
category, dual

00:20:14.030 --> 00:20:16.940
regional and
multiregional, which

00:20:16.940 --> 00:20:21.530
will store your data in such a
way that if a region goes down,

00:20:21.530 --> 00:20:26.730
then you can immediately
be able to continue

00:20:26.730 --> 00:20:31.830
to read data from another copy
of the data in another region.

00:20:31.830 --> 00:20:34.950
So providing RTO,
so Recovery Time

00:20:34.950 --> 00:20:36.330
Objective of zero on failure.

00:20:36.330 --> 00:20:39.240
So if a region goes down, the
multiregion or dual region

00:20:39.240 --> 00:20:43.140
is still available for
reads, and your application

00:20:43.140 --> 00:20:47.450
can continue serving as
though nothing has happened.

00:20:47.450 --> 00:20:49.010
Persistent Disk--
so Persistent Disk

00:20:49.010 --> 00:20:50.490
is our block-storage product.

00:20:50.490 --> 00:20:58.490
So here you're really looking
to attach a disk to a VM

00:20:58.490 --> 00:21:01.610
and run a whole range
of applications.

00:21:01.610 --> 00:21:03.950
Traditional sort of
three-tier applications

00:21:03.950 --> 00:21:06.890
or high-end database
applications

00:21:06.890 --> 00:21:09.020
or even Hadoop and
big-data applications

00:21:09.020 --> 00:21:11.860
sometimes run on this
for the performance.

00:21:11.860 --> 00:21:14.390
And with Persistent
Disk, today we

00:21:14.390 --> 00:21:16.670
have two tiers, which
is a standard tier

00:21:16.670 --> 00:21:18.830
and a high-performance tier.

00:21:18.830 --> 00:21:21.500
And over the last
year, we've steadily

00:21:21.500 --> 00:21:23.270
improved the
performance of those.

00:21:23.270 --> 00:21:26.660
For example, for
Persistent Disk SSD,

00:21:26.660 --> 00:21:28.490
we've steadily improved
the IOPS that's

00:21:28.490 --> 00:21:30.140
available for those
tiers from 60,000

00:21:30.140 --> 00:21:33.560
to 100,000 IOPS, all just
as part of the product that

00:21:33.560 --> 00:21:35.390
was available.

00:21:35.390 --> 00:21:41.780
Today, we're announcing
some new tiers for SSD.

00:21:41.780 --> 00:21:43.460
So feedback we
had from customers

00:21:43.460 --> 00:21:45.410
was that while
they really enjoyed

00:21:45.410 --> 00:21:47.600
the sort of easy-to-use
performance,

00:21:47.600 --> 00:21:52.060
there was a couple of
trade-offs that they

00:21:52.060 --> 00:21:54.060
found hard to make with
the product that we had.

00:21:54.060 --> 00:21:55.670
So what we want
to do is introduce

00:21:55.670 --> 00:21:59.750
a new tier that has
lower performance

00:21:59.750 --> 00:22:02.010
and is better priced.

00:22:02.010 --> 00:22:05.180
That's our new Balanced Tier
that will be available at $0.10

00:22:05.180 --> 00:22:08.208
a gig versus SSD's
current $0.17 a gig.

00:22:08.208 --> 00:22:10.250
And then we're also going
to introduce an Extreme

00:22:10.250 --> 00:22:13.250
Tier for customers,
typically high-end database

00:22:13.250 --> 00:22:17.510
customers, that want the very
highest performance starting

00:22:17.510 --> 00:22:20.997
with 120,000 IOPS and
eventually going up from there.

00:22:20.997 --> 00:22:22.580
And then across all
of these products,

00:22:22.580 --> 00:22:25.340
some things that are really
powerful about Persistent Disk

00:22:25.340 --> 00:22:29.800
is that it's just
really easy to use.

00:22:29.800 --> 00:22:31.920
The disks can be
dynamically resized.

00:22:31.920 --> 00:22:34.640
They can be easily moved
around between VMs.

00:22:34.640 --> 00:22:36.140
You don't need to
strike your disks

00:22:36.140 --> 00:22:39.600
or prewarm the disks to
get maximum performance.

00:22:39.600 --> 00:22:42.020
It's all there ready
and available on demand.

00:22:42.020 --> 00:22:46.250
One big thing we focus on very
hard in storage at Google Cloud

00:22:46.250 --> 00:22:48.180
is just making our
products really,

00:22:48.180 --> 00:22:50.570
really easy to use for
customers so that you

00:22:50.570 --> 00:22:53.630
can focus on building
the applications

00:22:53.630 --> 00:22:58.490
and adding value
to your businesses.

00:22:58.490 --> 00:23:00.530
Some of the capabilities
that we have,

00:23:00.530 --> 00:23:06.110
high availability, backup,
and DR. So high availability--

00:23:06.110 --> 00:23:08.480
you can create a
regional disk which

00:23:08.480 --> 00:23:11.180
creates a disk between
two different zones

00:23:11.180 --> 00:23:14.600
and create active-active
replications

00:23:14.600 --> 00:23:17.047
or synchronous replication
between those two disks, which

00:23:17.047 --> 00:23:19.130
is super important if you
want to be able to build

00:23:19.130 --> 00:23:22.400
an application that can
survive a zone failure

00:23:22.400 --> 00:23:24.680
and recover quickly on
the other side, typically

00:23:24.680 --> 00:23:26.310
for something like a database.

00:23:26.310 --> 00:23:28.757
And then we also have
controls for being

00:23:28.757 --> 00:23:30.590
able to take snapshots
to protect your data.

00:23:30.590 --> 00:23:32.730
So you can take a
disk, snapshot it.

00:23:32.730 --> 00:23:34.520
The data is stored in
a different medium.

00:23:34.520 --> 00:23:36.260
You can store that snapshot
in a different region.

00:23:36.260 --> 00:23:37.385
You can schedule snapshots.

00:23:37.385 --> 00:23:40.520
So it works well with
traditional backup and recovery

00:23:40.520 --> 00:23:42.860
work flows.

00:23:42.860 --> 00:23:44.550
And then Filestore.

00:23:44.550 --> 00:23:46.550
So as I talked about,
Filestore, this

00:23:46.550 --> 00:23:49.340
is for customers who are looking
for a traditional file share

00:23:49.340 --> 00:23:52.280
available as a managed service.

00:23:52.280 --> 00:23:56.840
Classic use case is
I want to share files

00:23:56.840 --> 00:23:58.520
across my organization.

00:23:58.520 --> 00:24:01.460
I might be sharing them for
video and image editing.

00:24:01.460 --> 00:24:05.430
Might be storing web content,
home directories, et cetera,

00:24:05.430 --> 00:24:08.660
or I might be doing large-scale
rendering, video and image

00:24:08.660 --> 00:24:09.810
editing.

00:24:09.810 --> 00:24:11.960
And so with Filestore,
we offer fully managed

00:24:11.960 --> 00:24:14.240
network-attached
storage available in two

00:24:14.240 --> 00:24:19.950
different tiers,
HDD and SSD tiers.

00:24:19.950 --> 00:24:24.090
Again, goal here is to
give really easy to use,

00:24:24.090 --> 00:24:26.030
easy to understand
price/performance

00:24:26.030 --> 00:24:27.620
with really great performance.

00:24:27.620 --> 00:24:30.770
Very easy to use with
both Google Cloud Engine

00:24:30.770 --> 00:24:35.600
for VMs and Google Kubernetes
engine for Kubernetes.

00:24:35.600 --> 00:24:39.770
Another announcement that
we're very excited about--

00:24:39.770 --> 00:24:42.610
less than a year ago, we
acquired a company called

00:24:42.610 --> 00:24:48.555
Elastifile which had a really
powerful set of scale-out file

00:24:48.555 --> 00:24:49.840
store--

00:24:49.840 --> 00:24:54.060
had a very powerful
scale-out file product.

00:24:54.060 --> 00:24:58.580
And we are really
excited to have that now

00:24:58.580 --> 00:25:02.330
launching as a new
tier of Filestore

00:25:02.330 --> 00:25:04.070
called the high-scale tier.

00:25:04.070 --> 00:25:05.660
And this will let
customers scale

00:25:05.660 --> 00:25:08.600
to hundreds of terabytes, tens
of gigabytes of throughput,

00:25:08.600 --> 00:25:11.300
hundreds of thousands
of IOPS, and really

00:25:11.300 --> 00:25:14.870
help customers with some
of those large-scale web

00:25:14.870 --> 00:25:19.240
and high-throughput
media-rendering batch

00:25:19.240 --> 00:25:19.880
workloads.

00:25:19.880 --> 00:25:22.440
So very excited about that.

00:25:22.440 --> 00:25:26.450
And then the final area I want
to cover is around transfer.

00:25:26.450 --> 00:25:29.547
So having all of these
capabilities in the cloud

00:25:29.547 --> 00:25:31.630
is great, but how am I
going to get my data there?

00:25:31.630 --> 00:25:33.830
So our customers come to us
with many challenges here.

00:25:33.830 --> 00:25:35.540
Sometimes they're
shutting down data centers

00:25:35.540 --> 00:25:36.998
and they want to
move to the cloud.

00:25:36.998 --> 00:25:38.482
Sometimes they want to do POC.

00:25:38.482 --> 00:25:40.190
Sometimes they're
capturing large amounts

00:25:40.190 --> 00:25:42.803
of data for analytic
workloads, IoT workloads,

00:25:42.803 --> 00:25:44.720
and they want to bring
that data to the cloud.

00:25:44.720 --> 00:25:46.280
And we have two
different products

00:25:46.280 --> 00:25:48.290
available for customers here.

00:25:48.290 --> 00:25:50.810
Transfer Appliance-- so
this is a physical appliance

00:25:50.810 --> 00:25:52.130
that you can order from Google.

00:25:52.130 --> 00:25:53.570
We will ship it to you.

00:25:53.570 --> 00:25:55.310
You can rack it in
your data center,

00:25:55.310 --> 00:25:59.660
wire it up, and just load
it up directly with data.

00:25:59.660 --> 00:26:01.790
And you can move
up to a petabyte

00:26:01.790 --> 00:26:06.200
of data on this single
appliance without having to send

00:26:06.200 --> 00:26:07.470
any data over the network.

00:26:07.470 --> 00:26:10.330
So this is physical
transfer of data.

00:26:10.330 --> 00:26:12.770
And then secondly, we also
have Transfer Service,

00:26:12.770 --> 00:26:15.980
which is a software solution
which will move data

00:26:15.980 --> 00:26:17.990
over the network.

00:26:17.990 --> 00:26:22.370
You can move it between
clouds, within Google Cloud,

00:26:22.370 --> 00:26:24.230
and then earlier this
year we announced

00:26:24.230 --> 00:26:27.260
that you could move
data from on premises

00:26:27.260 --> 00:26:29.590
to cloud by Transfer
Service on premises.

00:26:29.590 --> 00:26:32.460
So here you download an agent.

00:26:32.460 --> 00:26:34.970
It can help manage the amount
of bandwidth and network

00:26:34.970 --> 00:26:37.370
that you want to make
available to the transfer

00:26:37.370 --> 00:26:42.480
and then manage long-running
transfers of data to the cloud.

00:26:42.480 --> 00:26:44.348
So again, our goal
here is to really help

00:26:44.348 --> 00:26:45.890
customers make it
as easy as possible

00:26:45.890 --> 00:26:48.980
to bring your data to GCP.

00:26:48.980 --> 00:26:52.310
Finally, I really want to
highlight our partners.

